## Package comparison

The round-1 phase package remains aligned with approved Phase 1, and this rework stays within its file scope.

## Findings

### Critical — Wrapping startup in `Effect.scoped` does not make the daemon runtime Effect-owned

`music-sessiond.ts` now registers `server.close()` as a finalizer, which improves top-level cleanup, but every long-lived resource created by `startMusicSessionServer()` still lives outside that scope. Provider/socket adapters are not Effect services or layers; provider events and command/sample workers are not scoped fibers/streams; callback sets—not `SubscriptionRef` streams—drive replay; polling/reconciliation remain raw `setTimeout`; and active provider work remains uncancellable. The coordinator’s queue/refs are still manipulated through isolated `Effect.runSync` calls inside a mutable Promise/class lifecycle. The scope can call an imperative `close()`, but it cannot interrupt or supervise the daemon’s actual work as required.

`SessionCommandError` also remains a conventional class rather than the required schema-tagged Effect error. Phase 1 still lacks `Schedule`/`TestClock`, named provider/socket layers, scoped acquisition/finalization, and interruption-preserving error handling.

### High — Required Phase 1 behavior remains largely untested

The session suite remains nine tests and still lacks mandated coverage for late-subscriber status/state replay, incompatible hello and capability negotiation, duplicate/out-of-order responses, malformed/oversized third-client isolation, stale sample rejection, coalesced sampling, 3/5/8 scheduling under `TestClock`, queue overflow and continuation after provider failure, optimistic seek, reconciliation timing, disconnect/indeterminate commands, listener isolation, and exact-once scoped finalization. No test exercises the new daemon scope or signal/finalizer path. The coder result also continues to substitute `npm pack ./packages/music-core --dry-run` for the exact verify command, which has not passed as written.
